readings.c1s1:
¶
(''e'') A momentary manifestation or exhibition; an indication; a view; a peep; a glimpse.

{{smaller block|One ''touch'' of nature makes the whole world kin.
{{right|''Shak.'', T. and C., iii. 3. 175.}}
In the Trojan dames there are fine ''touches'' of nature with regard to Cassandra.
{{right|''Marg. Fuller'', Woman in 19th Century, p. 105.}}}}

'''11.''' A trait or feature; a prominent or outstanding quality or characteristic.

{{smaller block|Neither ill ''touches'' should be left vnpunished, nor ientlenesse in teaching anie wise omitted.
{{right|''Ascham'', The Scholemaster, p. 48.}}
But he had other ''touches'' of late Romans,
That more did speak him: Pompey's dignity,
The innocence of Cato, Cæsar's spirit.
{{right|''B. Jonson'', Sejanus, i. 1.}}}}

'''12†.''' Manner; style; bearing.

{{smaller block|A certain ''touch'', or air,
That sparkles a divinity beyond
An earthly beauty!
{{right|''B. Jonson'', Alchemist, iv. 1.}}}}

'''13.''' The skill or nicety with which a performer uses his instrument; the peculiar manner in which an author uses his pen, an artist his brush, or a workman his tools; characteristic skill or method of handling by which the artist or workman may be known; execution; manipulation; finish.

{{smaller block|Be of some good consort;
You had a pleasant ''touch'' o' the cittern once,
If idleness have not bereft you of it.
{{right|''Beau. and Fl.'', Captain, i. 3.}}}}

readings.c1s2:
¶
{{smaller block|The literary ''touch'' which it is so difficult to describe but so easy to recognise. {{right|''Nineteenth Century'', XXVI. 838.}}}}

14. In ''pianoforte-'' and ''organ-playing'', a method of depressing a digital or pedal so as to produce a tone of a particular quality. {{smaller|The varieties of tone producible on modern instruments by varying the method of manipulation are numerous and at first sight astonishing. Much of the variety and effectiveness of keyboard technique is due to the elaborate study of this subject. Touch is described by various qualifying words, like ''staccato, legato, cantabile'', etc.}}

15†. Make; style; sort.

{{smaller block|The capteyn sent certeyn of his meyny to my chamber . . . and toke awey . . . j. herneyse [harness] complete of the ''touche'' of Milleyn; and j. gowne of fyn perse blewe furryd with martens. {{right|''Paston Letters'', I. 134.}}}}

{{smaller block|My sweet wife, my dearest mother, and
My friends of noble ''touch''. {{right|''Shak.'', Cor., iv. 1. 49.}}}}

16. A thing, or a style of thing, involving the expenditure of a particular sum, or obtainable for such a sum: as, a penny ''touch''. [Slang.]

{{smaller block|Sept. 22. At night went to the ball at the Angel, a guinea ''touch''. {{right|''Sir Erasmus Phillipps' Diary'' (1720).}}}}

{{smaller block|Print my preface in such form as, in the bookseller's phrase, will make a sixpenny ''touch''. {{right|''Swift.''}}}}

17. A musical note or strain. [Rare.]

{{smaller block|Soft stillness and the night
Become the ''touches'' of sweet harmony. {{right|''Shak.'', M. of V., v. 1. 57.}}}}

18†. Attack; animadversion; censure; blame.

{{smaller block|I never bare any ''touch'' of conscience with greater regret. {{right|''Eikon Basilike.''}}}}

readings.c1s3:
¶
19†. Personal reference or allusion; personality.

{{smaller block|Speech of ''touch'' towards others should be sparingly used; for discourse ought to be as a field, without coming home to any man.
{{right|''Bacon'', Discourse (ed. 1887).}}}}

20. A touchstone; that by which anything is examined; a test, as of gold by a touchstone; a proof; a criterion; an assay; hence, the stamp applied by the Goldsmiths' Company to a piece of plate testifying to its fineness: as, a gilt piece of the old ''touch'' (that is, of the stamp formerly in use).

{{smaller block|Fynd foure freres in a flok, that folweth that rewle
Thanne haue y tynt al my tast, ''touche'', and assaie.
{{right|''Piers Plowman's Crede'', l. 537.}}}}

{{smaller block|A day
Wherein the fortune of ten thousand men
Must bide the ''touch''. ''Shak.'', 1 Hen. IV., iv. 4. 10.}}

{{smaller block|Your judgment, as it is the ''touch'' and trier
Of good from bad.
{{right|''Middleton'', Family of Love, Epil.}}}}

{{smaller block|Be of happy cheer!
For 'tis the nicest ''touch'' of human honour
When some ethereal and high-favouring donor
Presents immortal bowers to mortal sense.
{{right|''Keats'', Endymion, ii.}}}}

21†. Some stone of a very durable character, suitable for preserving inscriptions or for fine monumental work. {{smaller|The confusion between ''touchstone'' and ''touch'', of which former word the latter seems to be a variant, is due in part to the general inability of men (everywhere existing until very recent times) to distinguish one kind of stone from another, and in part to the confusion, dating back to a very early period, between ''basanites'' and ''basaltes''. See ''touchstone''.}}

{{smaller block|Those other glorious notes,
Inscribed in ''touch'' or marble, or the coats}}

readings.c2s1:
22. In ''ship-building'', the broadest part of a plank worked top and butt, or the middle of a plank worked anchor-stock fashion; also, the angles of the stern-timbers at the counters.—23. In ''magnetism'', the magnetization of a steel bar or needle by repeated contact with one or more magnets: ''single'', ''double'', and ''separate touch'' describe different methods.—24. In ''bell-ringing'', a partial series of changes.—25. Same as ''toccata''. [Rare.]—{{smaller|'''A near touch''', an exceedingly narrow miss or escape; a close shave. [Colloq.]}}

{{smaller block|The next instant the hind coach passed my engine by a shave. It was the ''nearest touch'' I ever saw.
{{right|''Dickens''. (''Imp. Dict.'')}}}}

{{smaller|'''Royal touch''', the touch of the king, formerly applied as a remedy to persons suffering from scrofula. See ''king's evil'' (under ''evil''<sup>1</sup>), and ''touchpiece''.—'''To keep touch'''. (''a'') To be or remain in contact or sympathy. (''b''†) To keep faith or one's appointment or engagement; fulfil one's duty or functions.}}

{{smaller block|They ''keep'' no ''touch'', they will talk of many gay things, they will pretend this and that, but they keep no promise.
{{right|''Latimer'', 3d Sermon bef. Edw. VI., 1549.}}}}

{{smaller block|If Florence now ''keep touch'', we shortly shall
Conclude all fear with a glad nuptial.
{{right|''Shirley'', Bird in a Cage, iv. 1.}}}}

{{smaller|'''True as touch'''†, completely true. ''Spenser'', F. Q., I. iii. 2.}}

'''touchable''' (tuch′ạ-bl), ''a.'' [< ''touch'' + ''-able''.] Capable of being touched; tangible. ''Science'',

readings.c2s2:
VII. 271.

'''touchableness''' (tuch′a-bl-nes), ''n.'' The quality of being touchable; tangibility.

'''touch-and-go''' (tuch′and-gō′), ''a.'' and ''n.'' '''I.''' ''a.'' 1. Of uncertain action or outcome; that may explode, go off, or come to a head on the least touch or provocation; hence, ticklish; uncertain: applied to persons, circumstances, or actions.

{{smaller block|It was, as Rochford felt, ''touch and go'', very delicate work with Sir Edward. {{right|''Mrs. Oliphant'', Poor Gentleman, xli.}}}}

{{smaller block|It was ''touch and go'' to that degree that they couldn't come near him, they couldn't feed him, they could scarcely look at him. {{right|''The Century'', XXXVI. 127.}}}}

2. Hasty and superficial; desultory.

{{smaller block|The allusive, ''touch-and-go'' manner. {{right|''The Academy'', March 3, 1888, p. 148.}}}}

'''II.''' ''n.'' An uncertain or precarious state of affairs as regards the happening or not happening of something.

'''touch-body''' (tuch′bod″i), ''n.'' A tactile corpuscle (which see, under ''corpuscle'').

'''touch-box†''' (tuch′boks), ''n.'' A primer.

{{smaller block|Cocke, thy father was a fresh-water soldier, thou art not; Thou hast beene powdred, witnesse thy flaxe & ''touch-box.'' {{right|''Heywood'', Royal King (Works, ed. Pearson, 1874, VI. 13).}}}}

'''touch-corpuscle''' (tuch′kôr″pus-l), ''n.'' A touch-body. See ''corpuscle.''

'''touch-down''' (tuch′doun), ''n.'' In ''foot-ball'', the touching of the ball to the ground by a player behind his opponent's goal; the play by which

readings.c2s3:
this is done.—{{smaller|'''Safety touch-down''', a touch-down made by one of the players behind his own goal when the ball was last touched by one of his own side. It is done for the purpose of preventing the making of a touch-down by the other side. See ''foot-ball.''}}

'''toucher''' (tuch′ėr), ''n.'' [< ''touch'' + ''-er''<sup>1</sup>.] One who or that which touches; specifically, a skilful archer; one who always hits the mark.

{{smaller block|Mammon, well follow'd? Cupid, bravely led;<br>Both ''touchers;'' equal fortune makes a dead.
{{right|''Quarles,'' Emblems, i. 10, Epig.}}}}

'''A near toucher''', {{smaller|a close shave. [Slang.]}}

{{smaller block|It was ''a near toucher,'' though.
{{right|''Sala,'' Baddington Peerage, I. 188. (''Hoppe.'')}}}}

'''As near as a toucher''', {{smaller|almost exactly; very nearly; touch-and-go. [Slang.]}}

{{smaller block|And there we are in four minutes' time, ''as near as a toucher.'' {{right|''Dickens,'' Our Mutual Friend, iii. 13.}}}}

'''touch-hole''' (tuch′hōl), ''n.'' A small tubular opening through the thickness of the barrel of a gun, cannon, or pistol, by means of which fire is communicated to the charge within.

{{smaller block|Love's fire-arms here are since not worth a souse;<br>We've lost the only ''touch-hole'' of our house.
{{right|''Farquhar,'' Love and a Bottle, Epil.}}}}

'''touchily''' (tuch′i-li), ''adv.'' [< ''touchy'' + ''-ly''<sup>2</sup>. Cf. ''techily.''] In a touchy manner; with irritation; peevishly.

'''touchiness''' (tuch′i-nes), ''n.'' [< ''touchy'' + ''-ness.'' Cf. ''techiness.''] The character of being touchy; peevishness; irritability; irascibility.

'''touching''' (tuch′ing), ''p. a.'' [Ppr. of ''touch, v.''] Affecting; moving; pathetic.

'''touching''' (tuch′ing), ''n.'' [Verbal n. of ''touch, v.''] The act of one who touches, in any sense.—'''Touching of St. Thomas.''' {{smaller|Same as ''Low Sunday'' (which}}

